An innovation-driven global pharmaceutical company, Zydus Cadila reportedly announced that its NEW Drug Application (NDA) has been approved by the Drug Controller General of India (DCGI) for Saroglitazar for the treatment and medication of Non-Cirrhotic Non-Alcoholic Steatohepatitis (NASH) in India.

&nbsp;

NASH is known to be a progressive disease of the liver caused by fat accumulation in the liver known as Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD). Such condition has the probability of leading to Cirrhosis and Liver Failure too. It is therefore a huge unmet medical need which doesn’t have any approved drug at present for its treatment anywhere in the world despite this disease being prevalent in 10% to 30% of the global population. Nearly 25% of the population in India has been estimated with the prevalence of NASH. NASH stands as one of the major causes behind Cirrhosis, behind alcoholic liver disease and behind hepatitis C. The only option for managing advanced cirrhosis with liver failure remains Liver Transplantation.

&nbsp;

It was in September 2019 when Saroglitazar got launched for the treatment of hypertriglyceridemia and diabetic dyslipidemia in patients with type-2 diabetes not controlled by statins alone. Saroglitazar in January,2020 received an approval for the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Over a million patients in the last seven years have benefitted from this drug.

&nbsp;

Saroglitazar is poised uniquely with tis PPAR alpha &amp; gamma properties which reduces the comorbidities (hypertriglyceridemia, dyslipidemia, diabetes mellitus) and causes NASH resolution. Zydus even achieved positive results in EVIDENCES II trial, a Phase 3 liver biopsy trial of Saroglititazar 4 mg vs. Placebo in Indian patients with NASH. Using liver biopsy by the end of 52 weeks and properly met primary as well as secondary endpoints, the trial evaluated histological improvement of NASH. Saraglitazar 4 mg hence demonstrated a noticeable reduction in liver fat, disease activity and liver enzymes.

&nbsp;

Saroglitazar in EVIDENCES 1 which was a Phase 2 clinical trial demonstrated improvement in liver enzymes as well as lipids in patients with NAFLD. On the global front, a phase 2 trial of Saroglitazar Mg (EVIDENCES IV) in patients with NASH in the US met primary as well as secondary endpoints. The results were shown at the Liver Meeting® 2019 which is the annual meeting of the American Association for the study of Liver Disease (AASLD) held at Boston.

15 investigators additionally initiated clinical studies of Saroglitazar got presented as well as published in leading scientific conferences and journals.

